# Additional Mandatory Requirement: Fully Responsive UI and Adaptive Sizing

Apply these requirements to the entire Brand Store Anand React frontend, including the public website, admin dashboard, product pages, modals, forms, tables, advertisement popups, and SVG preloader.

## 1. Responsive Design Principles

Build a genuinely responsive website, not a desktop design that merely shrinks on mobile.

Every page must automatically adapt its layout, typography, spacing, images, and interactive elements to the available screen size.

Support:
- Small mobile phones: 320px–374px.
- Standard mobile phones: 375px–479px.
- Large mobile phones: 480px–767px.
- Tablets: 768px–1023px.
- Laptops: 1024px–1439px.
- Large desktop screens: 1440px and above.

These are testing ranges, not rigid device categories. Use flexible CSS layouts and content-based breakpoints where appropriate.

## 2. Responsive Typography

All text must scale appropriately across screen sizes.

Use CSS `clamp()`, `rem`, and relative sizing instead of fixed pixel font sizes wherever practical.

Example:

```css
:root {
  --font-xs: clamp(0.7rem, 0.65rem + 0.15vw, 0.8rem);
  --font-sm: clamp(0.8rem, 0.75rem + 0.2vw, 0.95rem);
  --font-base: clamp(0.9rem, 0.85rem + 0.25vw, 1.05rem);
  --font-lg: clamp(1.1rem, 0.95rem + 0.6vw, 1.5rem);
  --font-xl: clamp(1.4rem, 1.1rem + 1.2vw, 2.2rem);
  --font-hero: clamp(2rem, 1.2rem + 4vw, 4.8rem);
}
```

Apply adaptive sizing to:
- Hero headings.
- Section headings.
- Product names.
- Product descriptions.
- Prices and discount labels.
- Navigation links.
- Buttons.
- Form labels and inputs.
- Dashboard headings and statistics.
- Table content.
- Modal text.
- Footer content.

Requirements:
- Prevent headings from overflowing their containers.
- Allow text to wrap naturally.
- Use sensible line heights.
- Avoid unnecessarily tiny text on mobile.
- Use truncation only when appropriate, with access to the complete content.
- Do not shrink all text uniformly; prioritize readability and hierarchy.

## 3. Responsive Layouts

Use:
- CSS Grid.
- Flexbox.
- `minmax()`.
- Relative widths.
- `max-width`.
- `min-width: 0` where needed.
- Fluid spacing.
- Responsive container widths.

Example product grid:

```css
.product-grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr));
  gap: clamp(0.65rem, 1.5vw, 1.5rem);
}

@media (max-width: 1023px) {
  .product-grid {
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
  }
}

@media (max-width: 767px) {
  .product-grid {
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 0.75rem;
  }
}

@media (max-width: 359px) {
  .product-grid {
    gap: 0.5rem;
  }
}
```

Adapt the layout according to the actual content and available space.

Do not force four product columns onto mobile screens.

Use consistent container widths, responsive horizontal padding, and balanced whitespace throughout the website.

## 4. Responsive Navigation

Desktop:
- Full navigation bar.
- Proper spacing between links.
- Search and contact actions.

Mobile and tablet:
- Compact logo.
- Collapsible navigation menu.
- Touch-friendly menu controls.
- No overlapping navigation items.
- No horizontal scrolling.
- Clear open and close states.

The navigation must work with touch, mouse, and keyboard interactions.

## 5. Responsive Product Cards and Images

Product cards must resize naturally without distorting product photography.

Requirements:
- Use `width: 100%` and suitable `aspect-ratio` values.
- Use `object-fit: cover` or `contain` according to the image's purpose.
- Keep product images aligned consistently.
- Scale product names, prices, badges, and action buttons.
- Prevent long product names from breaking card layouts.
- Maintain readable prices and discounts.
- Keep the NEW badge positioned correctly at every size.
- Use smaller card gaps on mobile.
- Avoid fixed card heights that cause text clipping.

Use responsive image sources where useful, including separate desktop and mobile hero images.

Product detail pages should change from a side-by-side gallery and details layout on larger screens to a stacked layout on mobile.

## 6. Responsive Hero Slider

The homepage slider must:
- Adapt to different viewport widths.
- Support separate mobile banner images.
- Use appropriate image aspect ratios.
- Keep heading and CTA text readable over the image.
- Reposition content when required.
- Prevent text from overlapping important image subjects.
- Maintain smooth transitions.
- Avoid excessive slider height on small screens.

Do not simply crop the desktop banner for every mobile screen when a dedicated mobile image is available.

## 7. Responsive Admin Dashboard

The admin dashboard must work on the designated management PC while remaining usable on tablets and mobile devices.

Desktop:
- Expandable or fixed sidebar.
- Main dashboard content.
- Responsive statistics cards.
- Searchable management tables.
- Comfortable form layouts.

Tablet:
- Collapsible sidebar.
- Reduced spacing.
- Flexible statistics grid.
- Responsive forms and tables.

Mobile:
- Collapsible navigation or drawer.
- Single-column or compact statistics cards.
- Stacked form fields.
- Touch-friendly actions.
- Horizontally scrollable data tables when necessary.
- Important actions remain accessible.

Never allow wide tables to force the entire page beyond the viewport.

Use horizontal scrolling inside the table container or switch to a stacked card layout where appropriate.

## 8. Responsive Forms, Dialogs, and Popups

All product forms, category forms, banner forms, advertisement forms, confirmation dialogs, and promotional popups must adapt to screen size.

Requirements:
- Full-width fields on narrow screens.
- Multiple columns only when sufficient space is available.
- Responsive image previews.
- Buttons that wrap or stack naturally.
- Modals constrained to the viewport.
- Internal scrolling for tall dialogs.
- Visible close buttons.
- No content hidden behind fixed headers or footers.
- Adequate spacing between touch targets.
- Keyboard accessibility.

The advertisement popup must fit small mobile screens without clipping its CTA or close button.

## 9. Responsive SVG Preloader

The Brand Store animated SVG preloader must scale proportionally across all supported devices.

Requirements:
- Keep the logo centred.
- Preserve the logo's aspect ratio.
- Scale the circular gold outline with the logo.
- Keep the loading bar within the viewport.
- Scale decorative elements and typography appropriately.
- Avoid clipping on short screens.
- Use a flexible SVG `viewBox`.
- Use CSS such as `width: min(80vw, 420px)` for the logo wrapper where suitable.
- Ensure the full-screen preloader adapts to landscape mobile screens.
- Preserve smooth animations at all sizes.
- Respect reduced-motion preferences.

Do not stretch the SVG or independently scale its width and height.

## 10. Responsive Spacing and Controls

Use shared spacing tokens and fluid values.

Example:

```css
:root {
  --page-padding: clamp(1rem, 4vw, 3.5rem);
  --section-spacing: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 5.5rem);
  --card-gap: clamp(0.65rem, 1.5vw, 1.5rem);
  --control-height: 2.75rem;
}
```

Ensure:
- Buttons do not overflow their containers.
- Inputs remain usable at small widths.
- Icons stay aligned with their labels.
- Margins and padding adapt to the viewport.
- Fixed and sticky elements do not cover important content.

## 11. No Horizontal Overflow

The entire website must not produce unintended horizontal scrolling at any supported viewport width.

Pay particular attention to:
- Navigation bars.
- Hero sliders.
- Product grids.
- Long product names.
- Tables.
- Modals.
- Images.
- SVG elements.
- Forms.
- Admin sidebars.

Use `min-width: 0`, `max-width: 100%`, appropriate wrapping, and contained overflow where needed.

Do not solve every overflow issue by applying `overflow-x: hidden` to the whole page; fix the actual layout problem.

## 12. Responsive Testing Is Mandatory

Test at these viewport sizes:
- 320 × 568.
- 360 × 800.
- 390 × 844.
- 430 × 932.
- 768 × 1024.
- 1024 × 768.
- 1366 × 768.
- 1440 × 900.
- 1920 × 1080.

Also test landscape mobile orientation and browser zoom where practical.

Verify:
- Typography and line wrapping.
- Navigation behaviour.
- Product card sizing.
- Hero image cropping.
- Forms and buttons.
- Dashboard tables.
- Modal and popup positioning.
- SVG preloader.
- No clipped content.
- No unintended horizontal scrolling.

## Final Responsive Acceptance Criteria

The website is not complete until the customer storefront and admin dashboard both work properly on mobile, tablet, laptop, and desktop.

All text, images, layouts, controls, and spacing must adapt automatically. The website must preserve the premium black-and-gold brand identity at every viewport size.

Responsive behaviour is a core requirement, not an optional final polish step.